    # P2's (records/logs/m1p5-p2/run.log) and part 6 P3's
    # (records/logs/m1p6-p3/run.log) scripts. It keeps their rules: lines 1 to 3
    # are the command, the invocation and "about:"; the shape greps with controls;
    # a root line for every root; the SERIALIZED line; no count the phase can grow
    # is typed, and every expected set is derived. What differs, and why:
    #  1. The phase, the log directory and the subjects are M2 part 1 P1's.
    #  2. This log builds the host release binaries itself ("the release binary
    #     at HEAD"): no size log exists in P1. It builds nothing else in release.
    #  3. The style-sample run's checks are part 5 P2's, carried over with its
    #     helper functions (copied, printed here) and its cmp.py (extracted,
    #     never retyped). Part 5 P2 ran both targets; this run is host only
    #     (P1 ruling 8), so its "two labels agree" block is dropped.
    #  4. The corpus is read and one run directory is written under it,
    #     <corpus>/runs/<utc-date>-<shortsha>, which must not exist at the start.
    #  5. The host keep, the list, the stage and the zip are on the disk under
